---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Install from '../components/Install.astro';
---

<Base>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="inner">
      <h1>Any agent. Any machine. One workspace.</h1>
      <p>
        Set up your projects, tooling and account connections once. Launch that
        same environment on any machine, hand it to whichever agent you like,
        and swap models — or go local — without losing any of it.
      </p>
      <Install />
      <div class="links">
        <a href="/docs/getting-started">getting started</a>
        <a href="/docs/manifesto">the idea</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sections">
    <section class="section panel notice" aria-labelledby="experimental">
      <h2 id="experimental"><span class="tag">experimental</span></h2>
      <div class="body prose">
        <p class="lead">
          suped is early, and it is not security-hardened. Treat it as a prototype.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your agent holds real credentials for real accounts. A container is not a
          security boundary against a capable attacker, and suped does not sandbox
          the agent from the services you connect to it. An agent that makes a
          mistake, or is talked into one, can do anything those credentials allow.
        </p>
        <p>
          Connect accounts you could afford to have misused. Prefer scoped tokens
          and separate accounts over production credentials, and give it the
          narrowest access that still does the job.
        </p>
        <p>
          Anything that lets outside content reach the agent — email, chat, a
          webhook — is a prompt-injection surface: text written by a stranger
          becomes instructions to something holding your keys. Those surfaces are
          not built yet, and this is the problem to solve before they are.
        </p>
        <p class="fineprint">
          Provided as-is under the MIT licence, with no warranty. Interfaces will
          change without notice while the project is at 0.x.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section panel">
      <h2>the idea</h2>
      <div class="body prose">
        <p class="lead">
          A headless operating system, run by a single agent of your choosing.
        </p>
        <p>
          During setup you pick the toolset your agent gets and connect the
          accounts you want it to use. What you end up with is a workspace you
          can launch from anywhere, carrying the projects, tooling and
          connections you set up.
        </p>
        <p>
          The environment grows with you. Start a project that needs tooling the
          workspace doesn't have and your agent installs it — and that addition
          travels, so your other machines gain it too. What began as a curated
          setup becomes yours.
        </p>
        <p>
          It stays fast by staying thin. There is no framework to learn and no
          preamble to read before acting: the operating brief is sixty-eight
          words, and everything else is an ordinary filesystem the agent reads
          when it needs to. That is what lets you kill a session at any moment
          and have a fresh one pick up where it left off.
        </p>
        <p>
          One agent runs the box, but that does not make it single-threaded. Your
          agent can install a multi-agent manager, or build one, the same way it
          installs anything else.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because the environment holds the tools, the accounts and the work, it
          does not matter much which model is driving. Change providers, or run
          something local, and everything you built is still there.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section panel">
      <h2>available now</h2>
      <div class="body prose">
        <p>
          Suped 0.2.0 opens a persistent Ubuntu workspace with guided tool setup.
          It needs Docker running and Node 18 or newer. The first run builds
          the image locally.
        </p>
        <p>
          Choose from 17 optional CLIs, connect your accounts, and add app
          connections through your agent's MCP client.{' '}
          <a href="/docs/getting-started">Get started</a> or{' '}
          <a href="/docs/getting-started#upgrade-from-010">upgrade your existing workspace</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section panel">
      <h2>set it up</h2>
      <div class="body prose">
        <p class="lead">Choose your tools. Connect your accounts. Hand it over.</p>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li><strong>Start the workspace.</strong> Guided setup opens on the first interactive run.</li>
          <li><strong>Choose your tools.</strong> Pick repository hosts, deployment platforms, databases, and an agent client. Connect the accounts and apps your work needs.</li>
          <li><strong>Give your agent the job.</strong> It uses those CLIs to work with repos, apps, and deployments, with the access you granted.</li>
          <li><strong>Come back to your work.</strong> Your home directory keeps projects, installed tools, and saved account configuration between sessions.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><a href="/docs/tools">See setup, account status, and reconnect commands.</a></p>
        <p>
          GitHub or GitLab. Cloudflare, Vercel, Netlify, or other hosts.
          Supabase, Neon, Turso, or PlanetScale. Add payments and cloud tools,
          then connect apps such as Notion, Linear, Figma, and Sentry through{' '}
          <a href="/docs/mcp">your agent's MCP client</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section panel">
      <h2>what's inside</h2>
      <div class="body prose">
        <p>
          Ubuntu, bash, Python, Node, Git, and uv. A normal filesystem, a user
          with sudo, and tools installed under your home. Nothing you have to
          learn.
        </p>
<pre><code>/home/suped/
  workspace/    where commands and shells start
  projects/     a place for your repos
  downloads/
  .config/
  .local/bin/   tools on PATH</code></pre>
        <p>
          Suped prepares the workspace. The agent uses ordinary programs and
          files inside it. You supply the agent and the objective.
        </p>
        <p>
          Home survives a container reset. System packages installed with apt do
          not. <a href="/docs/persistence">See exactly what survives what.</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section panel">
      <h2>hand it a job</h2>
      <div class="body prose">
        <p>After setup, a job can start with the services already connected:</p>
<pre><code>Build a project dashboard in ~/projects/dashboard.
Create a GitHub repo and deploy the app to Cloudflare Pages.
Return the repo URL and live app URL.
Save the code and run instructions in the workspace.</code></pre>
        <p>
          Setup can install Codex or Claude Code, or you can bring another agent.
          It prepares tools and account access; your agent creates the resources for the job.{' '}
          <a href="/docs/agents">Hand over the workspace.</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section panel">
      <h2>where it's going</h2>
      <div class="body prose">
        <p class="lead">Not built yet. Written down so you can see the shape of it.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Portability.</strong> A workspace is defined by its tool
          selection, its ports and mounts, and the repositories in it — all small,
          portable text. Carry that definition in git and a clone is enough to
          stand the same environment up somewhere else, built for the machine it
          lands on rather than copied byte for byte.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Identity that travels.</strong> Tooling and projects move today;
          account access does not, and reconnecting on every machine is the part
          that still hurts. Solving it without handing your credentials to anyone,
          and without requiring a paid service, is the open problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>More ways to reach it.</strong> Today the surface is a CLI.
          Email, chat, a local interface, a web one — each is a way to give the
          agent work and get something reviewable back. Read the note above
          first: every inbound channel is also an attack surface.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A human layer.</strong> Everything above describes a headless
          box. What is missing is the part where a person hands over work, sees
          what came back, and decides what happens next — from whatever device
          they happen to be holding. It is deliberately the least designed part,
          and it is what the rest is building toward.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .notice {
    border-color: rgba(255, 176, 120, 0.28);
  }
  .notice h2 {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .notice .tag {
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #ffc08a;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 176, 120, 0.42);
    border-radius: 0.35em;
    padding: 0.3em 0.6em;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .notice .lead {
    color: var(--fg-strong);
  }
  .notice .fineprint {
    color: var(--dim);
    font-size: 0.85em;
  }
</style>
